        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "This should now be resolved on latest `main`. Thanks again for the repro!\n\n---\n\nSo the general idea of what happened indeed seems to match the timeline in [this comment](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/issues/1890#issuecomment-3046835794). The main detail is that this happens if after showing popup with candidates the next *keyword* key hides the popup. This triggers `H.auto_info()` (because popup is still \"active\" just not visible; this is to be able to `<Del>` and still get to all candidates),  but `vim.v.event` is empty.\n\nImitating the \"show popup with one key and hide with next keyword character\" can be done more efficiently like this after opening `nvim` with only a single opened empty buffer:\n\n```lua\nlocal words = 'aa ab ac ad ae af ag ah ai aj ak al am an ao ap aq ar as at au av aw ax ay az'\nlocal n_char, delay = 0, 11\n_G.type_text = function()\n  if vim.fn.mode() ~= 'i' then\n    n_char = 0\n    vim.api.nvim_input('cc')\n  end\n\n  n_char = n_char + 1\n  local char = words:sub(n_char, n_char)\n  if char == '' then return end\n  vim.api.nvim_input(char)\n  vim.defer_fn(_G.type_text, delay)\nend\n```\n\nOne way to fix this is to just check `vim.fn.pumvisible() == 1` before showing info window. But a more robust approach seems to be to early return if there was another auto-info invocation in between.",
